 {
  "key": "exam.w1b-cambridge.q04",
  "type": "STRUCTURED",
  "concepts": [
   "amaths.series.geometric",
   "amaths.series.arithmetic"
  ],
  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 9,
  "estimatedSeconds": 810,
  "stem": "An arithmetic progression has first term $a$ and common difference $d$, where $a > 0$ and $d \\neq 0$. The 1st, 4th and 13th terms of the arithmetic progression are the first three terms of a geometric progression.",
  "parts": [
   {
    "label": "(a)",
    "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
    "marks": 3,
    "markingMode": "MANUAL",
    "stem": "Show that $d = \\frac{2}{3}a$.",
    "answerSpec": {
     "markScheme": {
      "points": [
       {
        "id": "p1",
        "description": "M1 uses $(a + 3d)^2 = a(a + 12d)$.",
        "marks": 1
       },
       {
        "id": "p2",
        "description": "A1 expands to $6ad + 9d^2 = 12ad$, so $9d^2 = 6ad$.",
        "marks": 1
       },
       {
        "id": "p3",
        "description": "A1 divides by $3d$ ($d \\neq 0$) to get $d = \\frac{2}{3}a$.",
        "marks": 1
       }
      ]
     },
     "modelAnswer": "$$\n(a + 3d)^2 = a(a + 12d) \\Rightarrow a^2 + 6ad + 9d^2 = a^2 + 12ad \\Rightarrow 9d^2 = 6ad\n$$\n\nSince $d \\neq 0$, $9d = 6a$, so $d = \\frac{2}{3}a$."
    },
    "explanation": "Three numbers in GP: (middle)² = first × third."
   },
   {
    "label": "(b)",
    "type": "NUMERICAL",
    "marks": 1,
    "stem": "Find the common ratio of the geometric progression.",
    "payload": {
     "answerPrefix": "r ="
    },
    "answerSpec": {
     "value": 3
    },
    "explanation": "$r = \\frac{a + 3d}{a} = \\frac{a + 2a}{a} = 3$.",
    "workedSolution": "1. $r = \\frac{a + 3d}{a} = \\frac{a + 2a}{a} = 3$."
   },
   {
    "label": "(c)",
    "type": "NUMERICAL",
    "marks": 2,
    "stem": "Given that $a = 9$, find the sum of the first 20 terms of the arithmetic progression.",
    "answerSpec": {
     "value": 1320
    },
    "explanation": "$d = 6$: $S_{20} = \\frac{20}{2}(18 + 19 \\times 6) = 10 \\times 132 = 1320$.",
    "workedSolution": "1. $d = 6$: $S_{20} = \\frac{20}{2}(18 + 19 \\times 6) = 10 \\times 132 = 1320$."
   },
   {
    "label": "(d)",
    "type": "NUMERICAL",
    "marks": 3,
    "stem": "With $a = 9$, find the least number of terms of the geometric progression whose sum exceeds $10^6$.",
    "payload": {
     "answerPrefix": "n ="
    },
    "answerSpec": {
     "value": 12
    },
    "explanation": "$\\frac{9(3^n - 1)}{2} > 10^6 \\Rightarrow 3^n > 222\\,223.2 \\Rightarrow n > \\frac{\\ln 222\\,223.2}{\\ln 3} = 11.21$, so $n = 12$.",
    "workedSolution": "1. $\\frac{9(3^n - 1)}{2} > 10^6 \\Rightarrow 3^n > 222\\,223.2 \\Rightarrow n > \\frac{\\ln 222\\,223.2}{\\ln 3} = 11.21$, so $n = 12$."
   }
  ],
  "explanation": "Translate 'terms in GP' into an equation in a and d, then use AP and GP sum formulas.",
  "hints": [
   "The 4th term is $a + 3d$ and the 13th is $a + 12d$.",
